\description{
Selects an \strong{information-theoretically optimal batch} of Active
Learning queries from a pool of candidates, following Kirsch,
van Amersfoort and Gal 2019 (see the @references section below).
Unlike the top-\code{n} BALD strategy (which
repeatedly picks the single most uncertain candidate and therefore
tends to select \emph{n} copies of "the same question" on clustered
pools), BatchBALD optimises the joint mutual information between
the \emph{batch} \eqn{y_B = (y_{x_1}, \ldots, y_{x_n})} and the model
parameters:
}
\details{
\deqn{
  \mathrm{BatchBALD}(B) \;=\;
    I\bigl(y_B ; \theta \mid x_B, \mathcal D\bigr).
}

For a regression model with Gaussian aleatoric noise of variance
\eqn{\sigma_a^2} and an epistemic posterior represented by \code{T}
parameter draws \eqn{f_\theta^{(1)}, \ldots, f_\theta^{(T)}}, the
objective reduces to a log-determinant (see the source comment at
the top of \code{R/active_batchbald.R} for the derivation):

\deqn{
  \mathrm{BatchBALD}(B) \;\propto\;
  \tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\!\bigl(
    \mathrm{Cov}_\theta\bigl(f_\theta(B)\bigr) + \sigma_a^2 I_{|B|}
  \bigr).
}

For a Quantile Regression Forest
(which is what \code{al_fit()} produces) the trees themselves are the
\code{T} parameter draws, so the joint covariance is just the per-tree
empirical covariance across candidates. The greedy selection
inherits the \eqn{(1 - 1/e)}-optimality guarantee of submodular
maximisation (Nemhauser, Wolsey and Fisher 1978) and is implemented via
Schur-complement / Cholesky updates so every greedy step is
\eqn{O(m^2 n_\mathrm{pool})} rather than \eqn{O(m^3 n_\mathrm{pool})}.

This is a \strong{complement} to \code{\link[=al_query]{al_query()}}, not a replacement: the
hybrid uncertainty + diversity strategy there remains the default
for low-budget settings where a physical-distance term is needed.
Use BatchBALD when (a) the covariate pool contains clusters of
near-duplicate candidates and top-\code{n} BALD would select all of
them, (b) the QRF aleatoric noise is well-estimated, and (c) the
batch size is moderate (\code{n <= 50} for laptop-scale pools of up to
~10 000 candidates).
}
\examples{
# `al_initial_design()` returns a bare integer vector of row indices
# into the pool -- not a list, so there is no `$idx` to pull out.
covs <- c("elev", "slope", "twi", "map_mm")
idx  <- al_initial_design(br_cerrado, covs, n = 30L, seed = 1L,
                          iter = 500L)
lab  <- br_cerrado[idx, ]
pool <- br_cerrado[setdiff(seq_len(nrow(br_cerrado)), idx), ]

# `al_fit()` requires `covariates`; `seed` is forwarded to ranger, so
# the forest -- and hence the batch selected below -- is reproducible.
fit <- al_fit(lab, target = "soc", covariates = covs, seed = 42)

batch <- al_query_batchbald(fit, pool, n = 8L)
batch                       # row indices into `pool`, in greedy order
head(pool[batch, covs])

# What BatchBALD buys over a plain uncertainty sort: the top-n
# strategy will happily return near-duplicate neighbours, while the
# log-det penalises them. Compare the *closest pair* inside each
# batch in standardised covariate space -- bigger is better spread.
greedy <- al_query(fit, pool, n = 8L, strategy = "uncertainty")
Z <- scale(pool[, covs])
closest_pair <- function(rows) min(stats::dist(Z[rows, , drop = FALSE]))
c(batchbald = closest_pair(batch), uncertainty = closest_pair(greedy))

# This 30-point fit is a runnable demonstration, not a usable model:
# its out-of-bag RMSE beats simply predicting the mean of the 30
# labels by well under a factor of two. A model worth acting on needs
# hundreds of labeled sites and spatially blocked validation; use
# al_loop() to grow the labeled set before trusting any batch.
c(oob_rmse  = fit$history[[1]]$rmse_oob,
  mean_only = sqrt(mean((lab$soc - mean(lab$soc))^2)))
}
